{
  "content": {
    "body": "A **workflow** pebble (the \"Application\" section — the app's structure and\nentry points) is a FLOW GRAPH: NODES (typed steps) joined by EDGES\n(directed arrows). It models a process — a function, a request lifecycle,\na domain flow — at whatever altitude you choose. This doc DEFINES every\nnode type and, crucially, WHAT CODE each represents, so a skill or agent\nreferencing it knows which node to use for a given piece of code. It is a\nreference for the doer, not the doer.\n\nTwo rules cover EVERYTHING, so nothing escapes:\n- If no specialized node below fits a piece of code, it is a **Process**\n  node, explained in that node's description. There is always a home.\n- Every path terminates at an **End** node — a normal return, an API\n  response, an error, an exception — all end at an end.\n\n## Node catalog — what each node is, the CODE under it, and its lines\n\nRead each as: what it represents · the CODE features that map to it · the\ninput/output line rules.\n\n- **Start** (`start`) — where the process begins. CODE: the entry point of a\n  function / method / handler / route action; not executable itself, just\n  the \"in\". LINES: 0 input · exactly 1 output. Exactly ONE per workflow.\n- **Process** (`step`, the default) — the CATCH-ALL for anything the other\n  nodes don't cover. CODE: a function call, a computation, an\n  initialization, a variable assignment, a middleware, a mapping /\n  transform, a service-method call, or any general logic you would rather\n  describe than draw out — plus anything not categorizable by the other\n  nodes (explain it in the description). LINES: many input · 1 output.\n- **Query** (`query`) — any database interaction. CODE: SELECT / INSERT /\n  UPDATE / DELETE, an ORM call (Eloquent / ActiveRecord / Prisma), a\n  transaction begin / commit, a connection, a raw SQL. Point the node's\n  refs at the model(s) it touches. LINES: many input · 1 output.\n- **Sub-Process** (`subprocess`) — an entire separate or LARGE process,\n  optionally LINKED to another workflow. CODE: a big piece of functionality\n  that is its own domain or layer — draw the top view here and link each big\n  piece to its own workflow (create the child first). Use it to keep one\n  flow at a single altitude. LINES: many input · 1 output. It MUST link a\n  child workflow (`subprocess { pebbleId, path }`); the CLI rejects an\n  unlinked one.\n- **Condition** (`condition`) — any branch. CODE: if / else, switch /\n  match, a guard clause, an error check (`if err != nil`), a permission or\n  feature-flag check, or a USER decision that changes the flow. LINES: many\n  input · MANY output — each arm a LABELED arrow whose text is the answer\n  (\"yes\" / \"no\" / a status / a case). Needs at least TWO arms.\n- **Parallel** (`parallel`) — work that happens SIMULTANEOUSLY. CODE: async\n  tasks kicked together (Promise.all, goroutines, threads, parallel jobs, a\n  fan-out). LINES: many input · MANY output (at least TWO simultaneous\n  branches).\n- **Loop** (`loop`) — any loop. CODE: for / foreach / while / do-while, a\n  map / filter / reduce over a collection, a retry loop, a poll. LINES: many\n  input · exactly TWO output — the **Cycle** line (into the loop body) and\n  the continuation (to the code AFTER the loop). The cycle path (which can\n  contain many nodes) MUST return to this loop node, or the node shows a red\n  invalid outline. The \"Cycle\" label is FIXED — not editable.\n- **Event** (`events`) — a domain event in the app. CODE: an event dispatch\n  / publish, a listener / subscriber, an observer, a broadcast. May be a\n  SOURCE (a listener with no start). LINES: many input · 1 output.\n- **Queue** (`queue`) — a queue / background job. CODE: enqueue a job, a\n  queued / background worker, a message-queue publish / consume. May be a\n  source. LINES: many input · 1 output.\n- **Cache** (`cache`) — a cache interaction. CODE: Redis / Memcached read /\n  write / invalidate, a cache-aside lookup, memoization, a TTL set. LINES:\n  many input · 1 output.\n- **Storage** (`storage`) — persistence to blob / file storage. CODE: S3 /\n  GCS put / get, a local filesystem write / read, a file upload / download,\n  saving a document or image. LINES: many input · 1 output.\n- **Timer** (`timer`) — anything time-driven. CODE: a scheduler / cron, a\n  scheduled command, a delay / timeout / sleep, a debounce, a UI delay. May\n  be a source (a scheduled kick with no start). LINES: many input · 1\n  output.\n- **API** (`api`) — external communication IN or OUT. CODE: an incoming\n  request (the endpoint receiving), an outgoing response, or a call to an\n  external provider — REST / gRPC / SOAP, an SMS gateway, a payment gateway,\n  a webhook, an AI service. It both RECEIVES and SENDS, so it is usually the\n  FIRST node after start (the request) and the LAST before end (the\n  response). It usually references a **structure** (the request / response\n  shape) via the node's refs. May be a source (an external API that injects\n  data into a listener with no start). LINES: many input · 1 output.\n- **Error** (`error`) — an error that occurs or is thrown. CODE: throw /\n  raise, an error return, an explicit failure path, a validation failure.\n  It is an OUTCOME: it flows into its OWN end node and is NEVER shared —\n  each error path gets its own error + end. LINES: 1 input · 1 output (to\n  its end).\n- **Exception** (`exception`) — an exception routed to GLOBAL handling.\n  CODE: an exception thrown WITHOUT a local try/catch, or one that the\n  global handler catches. It points at the global-exception flow. An\n  outcome → its own end. LINES: 1 input · 1 output.\n- **Global exception** (`global-exception`) — the ROOT of a SEPARATE\n  global error-handling flow. CODE: the app's global exception handler /\n  error middleware / panic-recover — how uncaught errors are handled\n  app-wide. Like start, it is its OWN little graph, NOT wired into the main\n  flow. LINES: 0 input · exactly 1 output.\n- **Try** (`try`) — the try of a try/catch. CODE: the `try {` opener,\n  marking where the guarded region begins. Partnered with a catch. LINES:\n  many input · exactly TWO output — one INTO the guarded body (whose path\n  returns to the catch) and one straight to the catch (the bracket spine).\n- **Catch** (`catch`) — the catch of a try/catch. CODE: `} catch {`, which\n  handles failure of the guarded region. LINES: exactly TWO input (one from\n  its try's spine, one from the body's last node) · exactly TWO output — the\n  **Exceptions** line (to exception / global handling) and the continuation\n  (to the code after the block). The Exceptions label is FIXED.\n- **End** (`end`) — the terminal. CODE: the return / exit of the flow.\n  EVERY path ends at an end — a normal return, an API response, an error, an\n  exception. Multiple ends are expected (each outcome gets its OWN dedicated\n  end). LINES: many input · 0 output. No node may be left without eventually\n  reaching an end.\n\n## Edges (arrows)\n\nAn edge is a directed line from a node's output to another node's input.\nMost arrows CAN carry a text LABEL — write on it what travels that line (a\ncondition's \"yes\" / \"no\" / a case / a status). The EXCEPTIONS, whose labels\nare FIXED and cannot be changed, are the loop **Cycle** line and the\ntry/catch **spine + Exceptions** lines.\n\n## Pebble-level components\n\n- **name** — the process / flow name.\n- **status** — spec↔code alignment (planned · in-progress · implemented ·\n  verified · drift · broken · missing · deprecated); each NODE also carries\n  its own status.\n- **description** — what the flow does and at what altitude / scope.\n- **references** — the pebbles the flow touches: **models** (from query\n  nodes), **structures** (from api nodes), and **workflows** (from\n  subprocess nodes). Wire these on the specific node's refs. A workflow does\n  NOT reference rules — rules are referenced only by skills and agents.\n- **resources** — code links to the files the flow lives in. Give the\n  workflow ONE resource PER meaningful node — the code link's `symbol` is\n  the function / method and its `note` names the node — so an investigation\n  opens the exact file for each node. (Node code lives in the workflow's\n  resources; a node's refs point at PEBBLES, its resource points at CODE.)\n\n## Node components — the categories under each node\n\n- **label** — the node's name (its box text).\n- **nodeType** — which of the catalog above (defaults to `step`/process).\n- **status** — the node's spec↔code alignment.\n- **description** — the detail behind the label; ESPECIALLY important on a\n  Process node that stands in for uncovered logic — say exactly what it is.\n- **refs** — the pebble this node concerns: api → structure, query → model,\n  subprocess → workflow.\n- **subprocess** — `{ pebbleId, path }` on a subprocess node: the linked\n  child workflow.\n- **enforcement** — only in rule-flavored flows; otherwise unused.\n- **x / y** — canvas position (let `arrange` place these).\n\n## Quick map — node → code it represents → lines\n\n| Node | Code it represents | In / Out |\n|---|---|---|\n| start | function/handler entry point | 0 / 1 |\n| step (process) | any logic not covered elsewhere (calls, init, middleware, transforms) | many / 1 |\n| query | DB read/write/transaction, ORM, raw SQL | many / 1 |\n| subprocess | a big/separate process → its own workflow | many / 1 |\n| condition | if/switch/guard/error-check/user decision | many / many (labeled) |\n| parallel | simultaneous async/threads/fan-out | many / many |\n| loop | for/while/map/retry/poll | many / 2 (Cycle + continue) |\n| events | event dispatch/listener/observer | many / 1 |\n| queue | enqueue/worker/message queue | many / 1 |\n| cache | Redis/memcache read/write/invalidate | many / 1 |\n| storage | S3/filesystem/file save | many / 1 |\n| timer | cron/schedule/delay/timeout | many / 1 |\n| api | external request/response/provider call | many / 1 |\n| error | thrown/returned error (own end) | 1 / 1 |\n| exception | exception → global handling (own end) | 1 / 1 |\n| global-exception | app-wide handler root (own flow) | 0 / 1 |\n| try | try { opener | many / 2 (body + spine) |\n| catch | } catch { handler | 2 / 2 (Exceptions + continue) |\n| end | terminal (return/response/error/exception) | many / 0 |\n\n## Workflow CLI — build a flow\n\nContent kind is **flow**: `nodes` (each { id?, nodeType, label, x, y,\nstatus, description? }) and `edges` (each { id?, source, target, label? }).\n`content` is written WHOLE. On create/update the CLI runs the full flow\nintegrity check (below), so a structurally-wrong flow is rejected.\n\nCREATE a flow (start → api request → query → condition → response / error):\n\n    pebbles-cli create workflows --title \"Create service request\" --content '{\n      \"kind\": \"flow\",\n      \"nodes\": [\n        { \"id\": \"s\",   \"nodeType\": \"start\",     \"label\": \"Start\",          \"x\": 240, \"y\": 40,  \"status\": \"implemented\" },\n        { \"id\": \"in\",  \"nodeType\": \"api\",       \"label\": \"POST /requests\", \"x\": 240, \"y\": 140, \"status\": \"implemented\", \"description\": \"Receives the create payload\" },\n        { \"id\": \"q\",   \"nodeType\": \"query\",     \"label\": \"Insert request\", \"x\": 240, \"y\": 240, \"status\": \"implemented\" },\n        { \"id\": \"c\",   \"nodeType\": \"condition\", \"label\": \"Saved?\",         \"x\": 240, \"y\": 340, \"status\": \"implemented\" },\n        { \"id\": \"out\", \"nodeType\": \"api\",       \"label\": \"201 response\",   \"x\": 120, \"y\": 440, \"status\": \"implemented\" },\n        { \"id\": \"err\", \"nodeType\": \"error\",     \"label\": \"500 error\",      \"x\": 400, \"y\": 440, \"status\": \"implemented\" },\n        { \"id\": \"e1\",  \"nodeType\": \"end\",       \"label\": \"End (ok)\",       \"x\": 120, \"y\": 540, \"status\": \"implemented\" },\n        { \"id\": \"e2\",  \"nodeType\": \"end\",       \"label\": \"End (fail)\",     \"x\": 400, \"y\": 540, \"status\": \"implemented\" }\n      ],\n      \"edges\": [\n        { \"id\": \"a\",  \"source\": \"s\",   \"target\": \"in\" },\n        { \"id\": \"b\",  \"source\": \"in\",  \"target\": \"q\" },\n        { \"id\": \"c1\", \"source\": \"q\",   \"target\": \"c\" },\n        { \"id\": \"d\",  \"source\": \"c\",   \"target\": \"out\", \"label\": \"yes\" },\n        { \"id\": \"f\",  \"source\": \"c\",   \"target\": \"err\", \"label\": \"no\" },\n        { \"id\": \"g\",  \"source\": \"out\", \"target\": \"e1\" },\n        { \"id\": \"h\",  \"source\": \"err\", \"target\": \"e2\" }\n      ]\n    }'\n\nLABEL an arrow — set `label` on the edge (a condition's answer, a status).\nNever set labels on the loop Cycle line or the try/catch spine/Exceptions\nlines — those are fixed.\n\nSUB-PROCESS — create the child workflow first, then a node that links it:\n\n    pebbles-cli create workflows --title \"Billing\" --content '{ \"kind\":\"flow\", \"nodes\":[ … ], \"edges\":[ … ] }'\n    # then, in the parent's nodes: { \"nodeType\": \"subprocess\", \"label\": \"Billing\", \"subprocess\": { \"path\": \"workflows/billing.pebble\" }, ... }\n\nLAY OUT — let the engine place nodes instead of hand-picking x/y:\n\n    pebbles-cli arrange workflows/create-service-request.pebble\n\nSTATUS per node · resources · inspect · validate:\n\n    pebbles-cli set-status workflows/create-service-request.pebble verified --unit q\n    pebbles-cli link-code workflows/create-service-request.pebble --file app/Actions/CreateServiceRequest.php --symbol handle --note \"Insert request (node q)\"\n    pebbles-cli refs workflows/create-service-request.pebble\n    pebbles-cli validate workflows/create-service-request.pebble\n\nThe validator enforces: exactly one start (0 in); every path ends at an end\n(no dangling; multiple ends expected); error/exception are dedicated\noutcomes each into their OWN end (never shared); loop has 2 outputs with a\nreturning cycle; condition ≥2 labeled arms; parallel ≥2 branches; try has 2\noutputs and catch has 2 in / 2 out with a matching try; global-exception\ntakes no input; a subprocess must link its child workflow.\n\n## Method lives elsewhere\n\nThis doc DEFINES the nodes. HOW to model a flow from code (altitude, mode,\nwhat to capture) lives in the **workflow-modeling** doc; proving it against\nthe code lives in **verification-model**. Both reference the nodes above.",
    "kind": "text"
  },
  "createdAt": "2026-07-27T19:10:37.183Z",
  "description": "The component CATALOG of a Workflow pebble (the app's structure as a FLOW GRAPH — the largest section): every NODE type with what CODE it represents and its input/output line rules, how EDGES/labels work, resources per node, the terminate-at-end / uncovered-goes-to-process rules, and the full workflow pebbles-cli usage. A referencing skill/agent reads this to know WHICH node a given piece of code maps to; it is a reference, not the doer.",
